package chrome

// UtilitiesRail is the right rail: `role="complementary"` and, exactly,
// `aria-label="Utilities"` (UI §4.5).
//
// # The rail persists last and collapses first
//
// That is UI §3.1's first rule and it is the reason for this component's
// per-tier behaviour. The rail is the utility panel; on the table route it holds
// the initiative tracker, which a GM needs *glanceable*, so it persists from 1280
// — the breakpoint chosen so both 1366 and 1512 get three columns — and every
// narrower tier turns it into something the reader opens deliberately:
//
//	below 640    bottom sheet, ≤70dvh, and ≤85dvh at compact-short
//	640–1279     right drawer, 22rem, with a scrim
//	1280 upward  persistent, 20rem at large and 22rem at wide, own scroll
//
// A bottom sheet below 640 rather than a side drawer, because §3.1's arithmetic
// does not allow otherwise: a 20rem right drawer and an 18rem left drawer
// together exceed a 360px screen by 250px, and one 320px drawer on a 360px screen
// leaves a 40px sliver and no way to see the page behind the scrim.
//
// # What is a slot and what is not
//
// The panels are the route's, not the chrome's: §4.4 gives this slot a different
// set per route, and a plugin page type fills the centre while the rail keeps its
// own. So the content arrives as a component and the landmark wraps it. §4.5's
// rule that a tier's panels are *absent* for a viewer below that tier is
// therefore the caller's, by omission: a panel nobody passes is not in the
// document, which is the stronger form of the rule and the one no CSS class can
// undo.
//
// Nothing here owns focus when the rail is a sheet or a drawer, and that is
// §4.5's other half: as a layer it traps focus, Escape closes it, focus returns
// to the trigger, and the page behind it is `inert`. The client owns that, and the
// hooks are already in the markup: `role="complementary"` is how head.js finds
// this element to re-parent into the sheet host at compact (§3.7), the
// stylesheet reads `aria-expanded` or `data-open` on the rail itself to decide
// whether it is off-screen, and the trigger carries the same `aria-expanded` so
// the two never disagree.
templ UtilitiesRail(panels templ.Component) {
	// `tabindex="-1"` so UI §7.2's utilities skip link lands on something a
	// screen reader announces, and `.target` because §10.6's audit walks every
	// element carrying a tabindex and this one is in that set. The class costs
	// nothing: the rail is 20rem wide and as tall as its grid area.
	<aside
		id={ railID }
		class="shell-rail target"
		role="complementary"
		aria-label="Utilities"
		tabindex="-1"
		data-shell-rail
		data-testid="shell-rail"
	>
		<div class="rail-panels" data-testid="rail-panels">
			@panels
		</div>
	</aside>
}

// UtilitiesTrigger is the control that opens the utilities rail, and it is
// exported rather than inlined because it does not belong to the rail.
//
// A trigger has to be reachable while the rail is closed, which means it cannot
// be inside it, and UI §4.1 does not assign it to one of the banner's zones —
// the banner's zones are campaign, search, connection, theme and user. The
// report names where it goes; this is the markup either placement needs, with
// `aria-expanded` already false, which is the state that works without a client:
// the rail's own stylesheet starts it off-screen at every tier below 1280, and a
// trigger that claims to have opened something is a focus stop that lies.
//
// Two placements, and both are §-consistent: the banner's right zone, or the
// compact bar's "More" destination. On the table route the compact bar is the
// player's action row instead, where the Token chip already opens the Tokens
// sheet — which is this rail.
templ UtilitiesTrigger(region string) {
	<button
		type="button"
		class="target rail-trigger"
		aria-expanded="false"
		aria-controls={ region }
		data-chrome="rail-toggle"
		data-testid="rail-trigger"
	>Utilities</button>
}
